  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "articles: a/an with countable food items (an apple, a salad)",
      "title_form": "Artículos a/an con comida contable",
      "title_target": "Articles a/an with countable food",
      "description_md_form": "En inglés, usamos **a** o **an** antes de un sustantivo contable en singular. Es como decir \"un\" o \"una\" en español.\n\n- Usamos **a** antes de palabras que empiezan con **consonante**: `a salad`, `a cake`, `a chicken`.\n- Usamos **an** antes de palabras que empiezan con **vocal** (a, e, i, o, u): `an apple`, `an egg`.\n\n**Importante:** No usamos `a` o `an` con sustantivos en plural ni con bebidas como `water`, `coffee`, `tea` o `juice`. Decimos `water`, no `a water`.\n\nEjemplos:\n- `Can I have a salad, please?`\n- `I want an apple.`",
      "description_md_target": "In English, we use **a** or **an** before a singular countable noun. It is like saying \"un\" or \"una\" in Spanish.\n\n- We use **a** before words that start with a **consonant**: `a salad`, `a cake`, `a chicken`.\n- We use **an** before words that start with a **vowel** (a, e, i, o, u): `an apple`, `an egg`.\n\n**Important:** We do not use `a` or `an` with plural nouns or with drinks like `water`, `coffee`, `tea`, or `juice`. We say `water`, not `a water`.\n\nExamples:\n- `Can I have a salad, please?`\n- `I want an apple.`",
      "grammar_structure": "a/an + singular countable noun",
      "conjugation_table": null,
      "examples": [
        {
          "id": "exm_gex_001",
          "lexeme_id": null,
